<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<databaseChangeLog
    xmlns="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog"
    xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
    xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog
        http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog/dbchangelog-4.29.xsd">

    <!--
      Phase 13 / audit §2.3. Two independent repairs to the role catalogue.

      ── 1. TENANT_ADMIN gets real administrative authority, WITHOUT acquiring TOTP step-up ──

      rbac.manage is the sole gate on every user-administration and branch-administration endpoint
      in user-service, and changeset 030 grants TENANT_ADMIN every permission EXCEPT rbac.manage.
      A tenant admin can therefore administer nothing, and "multiple admins per tenant" does not
      work at all.

      The obvious fix — grant TENANT_ADMIN rbac.manage — is the wrong one, and quietly so.
      AuthServiceImpl.requiresTotpStepUp returns true when the resolved permission list contains
      rbac.manage. Granting it would force mandatory TOTP on every tenant admin as an invisible
      side effect of an authorisation change, and a tenant admin with no enrolled secret would meet
      TotpEnrollmentRequiredException at login — the fix would re-break login for the exact persona
      it exists to enable.

      So the authority is SPLIT into three fine-grained codes. rbac.manage stays in the catalogue,
      stays granted to OWNER, and stays excluded from TENANT_ADMIN, so OWNER keeps step-up (which
      is the intended behaviour for the highest-privilege tenant role) and TENANT_ADMIN does not
      acquire it. The three new codes are deliberately NOT prefixes or suffixes of rbac.manage as a
      string: requiresTotpStepUp does an exact List.contains, and 'rbac.user.manage' is not
      'rbac.manage', so no step-up is triggered.

      These are point-in-time inserts on purpose. The blanket grants in 030/041/042/045/046 are
      also point-in-time: they enumerate the permissions that existed on the day they ran and never
      retro-grant anything added later. That is why OWNER is granted the three codes EXPLICITLY
      here rather than being assumed to inherit them.

      ── 2. The WAITER role ──

      There is no waiter persona. Table staff today must be given CASHIER, which carries
      pos.till.open / pos.till.close and pos.order.void.own — i.e. every waiter in the building can
      open and close the till and void their own orders. WAITER takes orders and nothing else.

      Every code below was read out of 030/041/042 rather than invented. An invented code produces
      a silently permissionless login and is the highest-recurrence defect class in this
      repository — see PermissionCatalogClosureTest for the five outages that had exactly that
      shape. WaiterRoleGrantsTest pins the grant set from the other direction: it fails if any
      future changeset grants WAITER anything from the till, void, refund, payment or discount
      families.
    -->

    <changeSet id="auth-1.0.0-055-tenant-admin-authority-split" author="restaurantos-agent" runOnChange="false">
        <sql splitStatements="true" endDelimiter=";">
            INSERT INTO permissions (code, module, description) VALUES
              ('rbac.user.manage', 'rbac',   'Administer tenant users (list, invite, update, deactivate, reset password)'),
              ('rbac.role.manage', 'rbac',   'Assign and revoke per-branch roles for tenant users'),
              ('branch.manage',    'branch', 'Create, update and deactivate branches')
            ON CONFLICT (code) DO NOTHING;

            INSERT INTO role_permissions (role_code, permission_code) VALUES
              ('OWNER',        'rbac.user.manage'),
              ('OWNER',        'rbac.role.manage'),
              ('OWNER',        'branch.manage'),
              ('TENANT_ADMIN', 'rbac.user.manage'),
              ('TENANT_ADMIN', 'rbac.role.manage'),
              ('TENANT_ADMIN', 'branch.manage')
            ON CONFLICT (role_code, permission_code) DO NOTHING;
        </sql>
        <!--
          TENANT_ADMIN is deliberately NOT granted rbac.manage here. That is the whole point of the
          split; granting it would silently force TOTP enrolment on every tenant admin.
        -->
        <rollback>
            <sql splitStatements="true" endDelimiter=";">
                DELETE FROM role_permissions
                 WHERE permission_code IN ('rbac.user.manage', 'rbac.role.manage', 'branch.manage');
                DELETE FROM permissions
                 WHERE code IN ('rbac.user.manage', 'rbac.role.manage', 'branch.manage');
            </sql>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>

    <changeSet id="auth-1.0.0-055-seed-waiter-role" author="restaurantos-agent" runOnChange="false">
        <!--
          uk_roles_tenant_code spans (tenant_id, code) and tenant_id is NULL for system roles.
          Postgres treats NULLs in a unique constraint as distinct, so ('NULL', 'WAITER') does NOT
          conflict with itself and ON CONFLICT cannot be used here — a re-run would insert a second
          WAITER row. WHERE NOT EXISTS is the guard that actually holds.
        -->
        <sql splitStatements="true" endDelimiter=";">
            INSERT INTO roles (tenant_id, code, name, is_system)
            SELECT NULL, 'WAITER', 'Waiter', true
            WHERE NOT EXISTS (
                SELECT 1 FROM roles WHERE code = 'WAITER' AND tenant_id IS NULL
            );

            INSERT INTO role_permissions (role_code, permission_code) VALUES
              ('WAITER', 'pos.order.create'),
              ('WAITER', 'pos.order.update'),
              ('WAITER', 'pos.order.view'),
              ('WAITER', 'pos.order.send_to_kds'),
              ('WAITER', 'pos.menu.view'),
              ('WAITER', 'pos.tables.manage'),
              ('WAITER', 'pos.kds.view')
            ON CONFLICT (role_code, permission_code) DO NOTHING;
        </sql>
        <!--
          WHY THESE SEVEN, AND WHY NOT MORE.

          The four the phase brief names outright: pos.order.create, pos.order.update,
          pos.order.view, pos.order.send_to_kds. On their own they do not add up to a working
          waiter, so three more are granted, each because a gate that already exists would
          otherwise 403 an ordinary part of taking an order:
            * pos.menu.view      — MenuController gates every read on it. Without it a waiter
                                   cannot see the menu they are ordering from.
            * pos.tables.manage  — TableController gates assign/release on it. Without it a waiter
                                   cannot seat a table or attach an order to one. CASHIER holds it.
            * pos.kds.view       — read-only kitchen ticket state, so a waiter knows when to run
                                   food. Not pos.kds.update: bumping tickets is kitchen work.

          DELIBERATELY EXCLUDED, and asserted excluded by WaiterRoleGrantsTest:
            * pos.till.open / pos.till.close / pos.till.reconcile.override / pos.till.review
              — the till is the cashier's. This is the entire reason WAITER exists.
            * pos.order.void.own / pos.order.void.any  — a void is a supervisor action.
            * pos.order.refund                         — money leaving the drawer.
            * pos.order.close                          — PaymentController gates taking payment on
                                                         it; a waiter does not settle bills.
            * pos.order.discount.*  / pos.order.split_bill / pos.menu.manage / pos.order.view.all
        -->
        <rollback>
            <sql splitStatements="true" endDelimiter=";">
                DELETE FROM role_permissions WHERE role_code = 'WAITER';
                DELETE FROM roles WHERE code = 'WAITER' AND tenant_id IS NULL;
            </sql>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>

</databaseChangeLog>
